==Latin==

===Etymology===
From {{proto|Indo-European|yē|already|lang=la}}. Cognate with {{etyl|got|-}} {{term|𐌾𐌰||tr=ja|lang=got}}, {{term|𐌾𐌰𐌹||yes|tr=jai|lang=got}}, {{etyl|goh|-}} {{term|ja|ja, jā|yes|tr={{etyl|de|-}} {{term|ja|lang=de}}|lang=goh}}, {{etyl|ang|-}} {{term|gea|ġēa|yea, yes|tr={{etyl|en|-}} {{term|yea|lang=en}}|lang=ang}}. More at {{l|en|yes}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|Classical}} {{IPA|/ˈjam/|lang=la}}
* {{audio|la-cls-iam.ogg|Audio (Classical)}}

===Adverb===
{{la-adv||iam|-}}

# [[already]]
#: ''Birota '''iam''' refecta est.''
#:: The bicycle has '''already''' been repaired.
# [[now]]
#* [[w:Curculio (play)|Curculio]] by [[w:Plautus|Plautus]], 5,3,5
#*: CAPPADOX: '''''Iam iam''' faciam ut iusseris.''
#*: THERAPONTIGONUS: ''Quando vir bonus es, responde quod rogo.''
#*: CAPPADOX: ''Roga quod lubet.''<ref>[http://www.thelatinlibrary.com/plautus/curculio.shtml Latin]</ref>
#*:: CAPPADOX: '''Here''', '''here''', I’ll do as you say!
#*:: THERAPONTIGONUS: Now that you are decent, answer me what I ask.
#*:: CAPPADOX: Ask what you like.<ref>[http://artemis.austincollege.edu/acad/cml/rcape/comedy/5-3.html English]</ref>
# [[anymore]]

====Usage notes====
{{term|iam|Iam|lang=la}} means, generally, “at some point previous” or “since some point previous”.  In English, {{term|already}}, the most common translation, is used only to emphasize that this point might have been expected to be later, whereas {{term|now}} is used to emphasize that the statement was once false, even when the statement refers to a point in the past or future.  {{term|iam|Iam|lang=la}} is used to express either.  (Likewise, the most common Latin word for {{term|now}}, {{term|nunc|lang=la}}, denotes only the literal present moment.)  Also, where {{term|iam|lang=la}} means {{term|now}}, it is often used in negative sentences, in which the most common English construction uses {{term|anymore}}.

